'''Machine Breakers''' were a widespread anti-industrial movement family that emerged during the first age of mechanised textile production and later recurred in several industrialising societies. The phrase is the English rendering of the Zong term used for the movement's earliest major outbreak. Although later observers grouped the regional movements together, they arose independently in different places and were not part of a single coordinated organisation. Their common features were opposition to mechanisation, fear of labour displacement, and hostility to the rapid replacement of hand production with machine labour.

The earliest major outbreak occurred in [[Zong]] in the 1790s, where textile mills and related workshops introduced machinery that reduced the need for hand spinners, weavers, and finishing workers. Initial protests focused on the destruction of looms and other factory equipment, but the movement quickly became broader than machine breaking alone. It attracted workers who saw mechanised production as a threat to wages, local custom, and craft-based livelihoods.

== Origins ==

The first Machine Breakers appeared in the textile districts of [[Zong]] during the 1790s, when mill owners began adopting machinery that increased output and concentrated production in fewer hands. Workers who had previously depended on hand labour faced reduced earnings, irregular employment, and the loss of bargaining power. Early protest actions were often local and spontaneous, but they created a pattern that later recurred wherever mechanisation advanced faster than wages or social protections.

The movement was never based on a single doctrine. In some places it defended established craft privileges; in others it expressed anger at merchants, mill owners, or state-backed industrial policy. What united the various movements was the belief that rapid automation was destroying established ways of life faster than communities could adapt to it.

